  "query_preview": "..Clear skies are in place across Utah and southwest\nWyoming as high pressure persists across the southwestern US. \nSimilar to last night, isolated areas of fog may develop late \ntonight in the Cache and Heber Valleys, though increasing high \nclouds will limit surface cooling and thus likely delay or even \nfully inhibit fog formation.\n\nOtherwise, expect dry conditions and another couple of degrees of\nwarming heading into tomorrow as high pressure builds. A dry\nshortwave trough will swing through the region on Tuesday,\nproducing slightly cooler temperatures on the order of a few\ndegrees. This shortwave, depending on its strength, could help\nventilate valley haze, but at this time it doesn't look like it\nwill be strong enough to fully mix out valley inversions. High\npressure will then return through at least the end of the week,\nwith high temperatures in the low-60s across most valleys.\n\nModel guidance continues to delay the return of active weather, \nwith next weekend now looking more likely to remain dry and \nrelatively warm. While there is still plenty of uncertainty with \nwhen this active weather arrives, models are in better agreement \nof mean troughing over the west coast by next Monday, indicative \nof a potential trend towards cooler, wetter (snowier) conditions.",
